92 Python modules, ran 131 subagents, and computed exact Ramsey numbers nobody had computed before. I mostly watched.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-16-computational-explorations/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Number-Theory</media:title></media:content><category>erdos</category><category>ramsey-theory</category><category>number-theory</category><category>sat-solving</category><category>survival-analysis</category><category>computational-mathematics</category><category>ai</category></item><item><title>Exact Rational Arithmetic</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-02-rational-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Tue, 18 Feb 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-02-rational-stepanov/</guid><description>Rational numbers give exact arithmetic where floating-point fails. The implementation connects GCD, the Stern-Brocot tree, and the algebraic structure of fields.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-02-rational-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Number-Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>number-theory</category><category>fractions</category><category>exact-arithmetic</category></item><item><title>Is It Prime?</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-09-miller-rabin-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Tue, 10 Sep 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-09-miller-rabin-stepanov/</guid><description>The Miller-Rabin primality test demonstrates how probabilistic algorithms achieve arbitrary certainty, trading absolute truth for practical efficiency.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-09-miller-rabin-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Number-Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>algorithms</category><category>number-theory</category><category>primality</category><category>probabilistic-algorithms</category></item><item><title>Modular Arithmetic as Rings</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-06-modular-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Sat, 22 Jun 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-06-modular-stepanov/</guid><description>Integers modulo N form a ring, an algebraic structure that determines which algorithms apply. Understanding this structure unlocks algorithms from cryptography to competitive programming.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-06-modular-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Number-Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>number-theory</category><category>rings</category><category>algebra</category></item><item><title>One Algorithm, Infinite Powers</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-03-peasant-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 Mar 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-03-peasant-stepanov/</guid><description>The Russian peasant algorithm computes products, powers, Fibonacci numbers, and more, once you see the underlying algebraic structure.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-03-peasant-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Number-Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>number-theory</category></item></channel></rss>